One of the most insightful videos I’ve watched from the latest @Davos Summit focuses on how to “control” AI. The speakers are true leaders in the field.
Here are three standout statements that I believe deserve careful thought:
@DarioAmodei (Anthropic): “It is my guess that by 2026 or 2027, we will have AI systems that are broadly better than almost all humans at almost all things.”
@Yoshua_Bengio (Université de Montréal): “Right now, science doesn’t know how we can control machines that are even at our level of intelligence.”
@YejinChoinka (Stanford University): “The fact that we know how to create that doesn’t mean that we actually understand it.”
These three statements serve as both a wake-up call and an invitation to act. As we continue to innovate, we must also prioritize discussions and strategies to navigate the challenges ahead. Regulating AI isn’t just a technical issue—it’s a societal one.
